-> State Management

Changing of current State
State management is how your app stores, updates, and uses data (state) across components

“State” = any data that can change
User login status
Form input values
Cart items in an e-commerce app
Theme (dark/light mode)

const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
count = current state
setCount = function to update state
When state changes → UI updates automatically

Built-in (simple apps): React’s useState, useContext from React

External state management tools
Used in larger apps:
Redux → very popular, predictable
Zustand → simpler, modern
Recoil → flexible, React-focused
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Routing – “Which page to show”
Routing decides what the user sees based on the URL


State Management – “What data to show”
State management controls the data your components display
-> Logged-in user info
-> Shopping cart items
-> Form inputs
Local: useState, useContext
Global: Redux, Zustand


RESTful API – “Where the data comes from”
RESTful APIs provide data from the server.
-> GET /api/products → fetch product list
-> POST /api/cart → add item to cart
Frontend fetches data and stores it in state.


Example Flow: E-commerce Cart
URL: /cart → routing loads CartPage
CartPage checks cart state
If cart state empty → fetch GET /api/cart
State updated → UI shows products in cart
User clicks “Remove” → state updates and API call DELETE /api/cart/:id

Summary
Routing → “Which component/page to show?”
State management → “What data is shown and updated?”
RESTful API → “Where the data comes from or goes to?”
They work as a team to make modern apps interactive and dynamic
